 "anatomy-steps": {
  "card/card-1": {
   "steps": [
    {
     "kind": "answer",
     "scope": "Elevation",
     "question": "What rises above the page?",
     "answer": "Only what floats",
     "why": "Only menus and dialogs float."
    },
    {
     "kind": "answer",
     "scope": "Elevation",
     "question": "What separates groups on the page?",
     "answer": "Space",
     "why": "Space separates groups; lines would interrupt reading."
    },
    {
     "kind": "answer",
     "scope": "Shape",
     "question": "What should control corners say?",
     "answer": "Rounded",
     "why": "Rounded corners feel friendly."
    },
    {
     "kind": "rule",
     "situation": "a card on the page",
     "use": "A border, no shadow",
     "not": "a strong shadow",
     "why": "Cards stay flat on the page; shadows are for floating things."
    },
    {
     "kind": "token",
     "semantic": "color.surface.default",
     "key": "background",
     "value": "#FFFFFF",
     "darkValue": "#1F1B16",
     "byScheme": true,
     "rule": "The base fill for content.",
     "pick": "neutral.0: the base for content; body text reads 17.12:1 on it, above the 4.5:1 floor.",
     "darkPick": "neutral.50: the base for content; body text reads 16.84:1 on it, above the 4.5:1 floor.",
     "shares": [
      "surface.raised",
      "action.secondary"
     ]
    },
    {
     "kind": "token",
     "semantic": "radius.surface",
     "key": "radius",
     "value": "0.75rem",
     "byScheme": true,
     "pick": "radius.12: We use a 12px corner for article cards, menus and dialogs in order to make each card read as a separate object, in a warm tone by brand convention. A larger radius does this by breaking the outline with a curve wider than the 8px controls inside it. So a card reads as one calm object on the page, because containers get their own corner (shape.roles, shape.count) and the brand is warm (brand.q1).",
     "shares": []
    },
    {
     "kind": "token",
     "semantic": "space.inset.container",
     "key": "padding",
     "value": "1.5rem",
     "byScheme": true,
     "pick": "space.24",
     "shares": []
    },
    {
     "kind": "token",
     "semantic": "color.border.default",
     "key": "border",
     "value": "#E4DDD0",
     "darkValue": "#4A4339",
     "byScheme": true,
     "rule": "Quiet edges and dividers. Decorative, so it has no contrast minimum.",
     "pick": "neutral.200: a quiet line that stays visible on every surface it meets (lowest 1.33:1, on surface.canvas). It decorates, so it holds no contrast minimum.",
     "darkPick": "neutral.200: a quiet line that stays visible on every surface it meets (lowest 1.76:1, on surface.default). It decorates, so it holds no contrast minimum.",
     "shares": [
      "surface.pressed",
      "disabled.border"
     ]
    },
    {
     "kind": "token",
     "semantic": "border-width.default",
     "key": "border-width",
     "value": "1px",
     "byScheme": true,
     "pick": "border-width.1",
     "shares": []
    },
    {
     "kind": "token",
     "semantic": "elevation.card",
     "key": "shadow",
     "value": "0px 0px 0px, #00000000",
     "byScheme": true,
     "pick": "shadow.none: We use no shadow for cards in order to keep them at the page's height: on the scale only floating layers rise. The missing shade does this by leaving the card flat, set apart by space. So reading flows down the page without boxes that lift, because only menus and dialogs float (elevation.roles) and shadows are soft (elevation.values).",
     "shares": []
    }
   ]
  },
  "card/card-2": {
   "steps": [
    {
     "kind": "answer",
     "scope": "Typography",
     "question": "Which text roles does it need?",
